<CaseLayout title="Astros repeat-ticket analysis" eyebrow="03 / ASTROS TICKETING · ANALYST CASE STUDY" heading="What brings a fan back?" description="Turning fan-experience survey data into a focused repeat-ticket-purchase experiment." role="Python data preparation, operational-driver comparisons, logistic regression, and an executive presentation." status="Completed analyst exercise / proposed operational pilot" stack="Python · pandas · NumPy · SciPy · Matplotlib · Excel">
  <section id="question"><h2>Find a useful place to start.</h2><p>“Improve the fan experience” is a broad ambition. This case asks a more actionable question: which operational area should leadership investigate first when trying to understand repeat ticket purchases?</p><p>I worked with the supplied case data to separate an interesting association from a defensible next experiment.</p><div class="metric-grid"><div><strong>1,800</strong><span>Surveys in the supplied case data</span></div><div><strong>462</strong><span>Observed repurchasers</span></div><div><strong>25.7%</strong><span>Observed repurchase rate</span></div></div></section>
  <section id="approach"><h2>Clean joins. Clear decisions.</h2><p>I validated a many-to-one survey/game join, with every survey matched to a game. Ratings were grouped by operational area, then compared across higher and lower experience ratings.</p><p>A logistic regression examined the pattern while controlling for ticket spend, game result, and run differential. I excluded overall satisfaction and stated purchase intent from the operational-driver model: the question was which controllable area to investigate.</p><figure><img src={href('media/astros-category-lift.png')} alt="Aggregate chart comparing differences in observed repurchase rates for higher versus lower ratings across fan-experience categories" loading="lazy" width="1420" height="799"/><figcaption>Observed differences in repeat-purchase rates between higher and lower ratings. Directional associations in the case data; no causal effect is established.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section id="evidence"><h2>A signal worth testing.</h2><p>Respondents with average concessions ratings of at least 4 repurchased at 28.0%, compared with 20.3% below 4. That is a 7.7-percentage-point association.</p><div class="comparison-bars" aria-label="Observed repurchase: higher concessions ratings 28.0 percent; lower ratings 20.3 percent"><div><span>Concessions rating ≥ 4</span><strong>28.0%</strong><i style="--bar:93.3%"></i></div><div><span>Concessions rating &lt; 4</span><strong>20.3%</strong><i style="--bar:67.7%"></i></div></div><figure><img src={href('media/astros-item-lift.png')} alt="Aggregate item-level fan-experience associations with observed repeat purchase" loading="lazy" width="1420" height="740"/><figcaption>Item-level associations help narrow the operational hypothesis. These are observed comparisons, not achieved improvements.</figcaption></figure><div class="evidence-note"><strong>The recommendation</strong><p>Prioritize a concessions value and cleanliness pilot. Compare subsequent repurchase with a holdout or matched group before treating the observed relationship as an intervention effect.</p></div></section>
  <section id="limitations"><h2>An association is not a sales lift.</h2><p>The analysis is observational and based on supplied case data. Unmeasured differences between respondents can influence the result. A pilot needs a defined intervention, comparison group, follow-up period, and outcome before its impact can be evaluated.</p><p>This was an analyst exercise. It does not imply an Astros client engagement or a demonstrated revenue increase.</p></section>
  <section id="artifacts"><h2>Inspect the aggregate evidence.</h2><p>The public artifacts contain aggregate charts only. Respondent-level case data is not published here.</p><div class="resource-links"><a class="button button-dark" href={href('media/astros-category-lift.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Category comparison ↗</a><a class="button" href={href('media/astros-item-lift.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Item comparison ↗</a></div></section>
</CaseLayout>
